      "explain": "Rereading makes the page feel familiar and lets you name things while looking at them. That is retrieval strength plus recognition. It never forces the names to be rebuilt from nothing, so storage strength barely moves, and a week later there is little left to retrieve. Option A has the two strengths backwards; the names were never stored well. Option C is the intuition the lesson argues against; more rereading mostly adds more fluency. Option D sounds like a mechanism but is not one; she can picture the page because recognising a familiar layout is easy, not because the pictures were stored in some way the labels were not."
