      "explain": "Carpenter and colleagues' rule of thumb (2012) gives 10–20% of 70 days, which is 7 to 14 days. Cepeda's own data point for a 70-day horizon (2008) put the best gap at 21 days. So anything from one to three weeks sits inside the evidence, and because undershooting cost far more than overshooting in that study, the safe error is the longer one. Daily review is shorter than the evidence supports for anything beyond a one-week horizon: nothing fades between reviews, so each one is recognition rather than reconstruction. Four days copies a gap chosen for a different horizon; the gap should grow with the horizon. Five weeks is past every observed optimum for this delay, though it would hurt you less than daily review would."
