      "answer": 2,
      "explain": "For a horizon measured in years, the gap grows into months. Bahrick's nine-year study (1993) found that thirteen relearning sessions 56 days apart gave retention comparable to twenty-six sessions 14 days apart, so wide gaps bought the same memory for half the sessions. Bahrick (1984) found the loss after study stops is front-loaded, over the first 3–6 years, and what survives that phase holds for decades. So the deliberate schedule belongs in the first few years, with each session a retrieval session, and after that ordinary use will do. Daily review for a year is far shorter than optimal and then stops exactly when the steep decline begins. Weekly is also shorter than the evidence supports at this horizon and costs twice the sessions for the same result. Waiting five years lets the steep decline run its course before you start."
