      "explain": "In the first experiment (120 people, each seeing some artists blocked and some interleaved), interleaved artists were identified at .61 against .35 for blocked ones, and 78% of participants did better on the interleaved artists. Asked which had helped more, 78% said blocking was as good or better. In a second experiment (80 people), of the 72 who didn't say \"about the same\", 64 said blocking. So C. Nobody was shown scores by condition, and a follow-up found people couldn't even tell which artists had been blocked, which is why B is half right and still wrong: they couldn't tell the schedules apart, yet they had a firm preference, and it was for the one that worked worse. A assumes taking a test educates the judgement; it doesn't, because the judgement is built from how study felt while you were doing it (blocked feels smooth, interleaved feels hard). D invents a split the study never asked about. The practical lesson: don't use \"which felt better\" as your guide. Use a delayed test."
