      "answer": 1,
      "explain": "The 10–20% rule of thumb comes from Carpenter, Cepeda, Rohrer, Kang and Pashler (2012); applied to 30 days it gives 3 to 6 days. Cepeda et al. (2008), the data behind it, measured optimal gaps of 1, 11, 21 and 21 days for horizons of 7, 35, 70 and 350 days, so the nearest measured horizon to Nadia's gave 11 days. Between the rule and the data, 4 to 10 days is the evidence-based band, so B. Cepeda also found that undershooting the gap cost far more than overshooting it, so if in doubt, go longer. A is the \"never let yourself forget\" instinct; a one-day gap was best only for a test one week away. C is beyond anything observed: no optimum was above about a third of the interval, and she'd fit in only one review. D is wrong because Cepeda et al. (2006), across 317 experiments, found that the size of the gap matters; the count alone doesn't settle it."
