      "explain": "A partly forgotten memory has to be rebuilt on review, and rebuilding is retrieval practice, one of the two best-supported techniques in the field (spacing is the other). A fresh memory is recognised, and recognition doesn't exercise the route. That is the best-supported account of why spacing works, though researchers still argue about the mechanism. Either way, the data say the gap should be long enough to let some forgetting happen: in Cepeda et al. (2008) a one-day gap was best only for a test one week away, and too-short gaps cost more than too-long ones. So A. C treats forgetting as failure. B would have raised fluency in session one and changed little at a week. D invents a complexity rule; Cepeda's data come from verbal material in general, simple lists included but not limited to them."
