      "answer": 3,
      "explain": "Bahrick, Bahrick, Bahrick and Bahrick (1993) followed four people (the authors themselves) for nine years: 13 relearning sessions at 56-day intervals gave retention comparable to 26 sessions at 14-day intervals, though the wider gap slowed the relearning slightly. So D. Four people is a small study and you should hold the number loosely, but it points the same way as everything else about long horizons: a comparable result for half the sessions. A counts sessions instead of gaps. B overshoots; wider is not always better, and Bahrick's wide-gap condition matched rather than beat the closer one. C misreads Bahrick (1984): in a cross-sectional study of 733 people, school Spanish declined for the first three to six years and then held nearly unchanged for up to 30, the \"permastore\". What predicted how much survived was mainly the original level of training; later rehearsal had little effect."
