      "explain": "This is the pattern from Rohrer and Taylor (2007), whose four-solids experiment had 18 students: blocked practice looked better during the session (about 89% vs 60% correct) and collapsed a week later (20% vs 63% for interleaved, d = 1.34). That's a small study, but the preregistered trial across 54 seventh-grade classes found the same pattern at one month, 61% vs 38%, d = 0.83 (Rohrer et al. 2020). So B. The mechanism: blocked practice lets you skip the hardest step, deciding which method applies, because the worksheet tells you. Interleaving forces that choice every time. A mistakes in-session performance for learning (lesson 1 again). C assumes order doesn't matter. D imagines the benefit is confined to mixed problems; it shows up on ordinary problems because choosing the method is the skill being tested."
