      "explain": "Brunmair and Richter (2019) pooled 59 studies: g = 0.42 overall, but 0.67 for paintings, 0.34 for maths, mixed and nonsignificant for expository text, and −0.39 for their \"words\" category (names in conceptual categories, pronunciation rules, translations), where blocking won. The closest thing to Ana's vocabulary plan, Hausman and Kornell (2014) mixing anatomy terms with Indonesian words, found no reliable effect either way. Interleaving works by forcing you to discriminate between confusable categories or choose between similar methods. Unrelated words and separate history chapters give you nothing to discriminate, so C. A, B and D each treat interleaving as a slogan rather than a mechanism with a boundary, which is why Dunlosky et al. (2013) rated it only moderate: a small literature \"with enough null effects to raise concern\". What Ana should interleave is confusable things: three French tenses, three chart types, three formulas."
