      "explain": "Ericsson, Krampe and Tesch-Römer (1993) reported that the best violinists had accumulated about 7,410 hours of solitary practice by 18, against 3,420 for the future teachers, and over 10,000 by 20. Those are averages, and Ericsson himself rejected the threshold reading; \"10,000 hours\" is Gladwell's. Macnamara, Hambrick and Oswald (2014) found practice explains 26% of performance variance in games, 21% in music, 18% in sports, 4% in education and under 1% in professions (that last figure rests on only seven effect sizes and isn't significant). Ericsson and Harwell (2019) replied that the meta-analysis counted almost any practice, not deliberate practice as defined. The preregistered replication with 39 Cleveland violinists (Macnamara and Maitra 2019) found the best had practised somewhat less than the good (8,224 vs 9,844 hours by 18), a difference that was not statistically significant, with practice explaining about 26% of variance against the 48% they computed from the original data. That is a live, contested question, so C. A and B each pick a side and call it settled. D overstates both ends."
